      "OverallSummary": "The case involves Care Obligation violations by Haywood Securities (USA) Inc. related to their insufficient due diligence on Canadian private placement offerings recommended to U.S. customers. From September 2014 to February 2023, the firm recommended 134 sales totaling almost $11 million of 53 different Canadian non-brokered private placements (NBPPs) without conducting reasonable due diligence. The violation was determined through the following analytical steps: (1) The firm failed to independently verify issuer claims and material representations; (2) Due diligence was limited to searching and reviewing public filings with little to no independent verification; (3) The firm did not conduct investigations about litigation history, disciplinary problems, or review of key contracts; (4) Despite written supervisory procedures requiring it, the firm did not conduct searches of regulatory history of issuer officers/directors; (5) The firm failed to maintain evidence or documentation of its due diligence efforts. Prior to June 30, 2020, these failures violated FINRA Rule 2111's reasonable-basis obligation. After June 30, 2020, they violated Regulation Best Interest's Care Obligation which requires broker-dealers to exercise reasonable diligence to understand potential risks, rewards, and costs associated with recommendations. Additionally, the firm failed to file offering documents with FINRA for 236 Canadian private placement offerings, violating FINRA Rules 5123 and 2010. As a result of these violations, Haywood Securities was censured, fined $175,000, and required to undertake remediation of its supervisory systems."
